//# #2745: a DESTRUCTURING pattern's payloads are paired against the scrutinee.
//
// Codex, P1 round 5 on #2761. Only a whole-value `PBind` carried the scrutinee's
// formal; every other pattern bound each payload binder to "", so the refusal
// declined on a value that IS erased. Measured:
// `"\{match (a, 1) { (b, _) => b }}"` with `a: T` printed `252`, the tagged
// pointer -- the same number the unwrapped spelling printed before this PR.
//
// A payload's type is not knowable in general: this pass has no types, and a
// scrutinee that is a name or a call says nothing about its pieces. It IS
// knowable when the scrutinee is WRITTEN AT THE SITE in the shape the pattern
// takes apart, so a tuple literal against `PTuple`, a constructor call against
// `PCtor`, and a record literal against `PStruct` pair positionally. Anything
// else still blanks, which shadows.
//
// The reverse error lives on the green side, where the refusal fixtures cannot
// reach it: a destructuring match at a CONCRETE scrutinee must keep rendering.
// `fixtures/lambda_bound_toplevel_witness_test.vibe` holds those rows.

struct Qt {
  w: Int
}

fn outer[T](x: T) -> String {
  let inner = [T](a: T) -> String {
    "\{match (a, 1) { (b, _) => b }}"
  }
  inner(Qt::{ w: 5 })
}

export fn _start() -> Int {
  String::length(outer(7))
}
